\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Write $S^{n-1}=\{x\in\mathbb R^n:\lVert x\rVert=1\}$, with the Euclidean inner product $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle$. The kissing number is
\[
\tau_n=\max\{|C|:C\subset S^{n-1},\ \langle x,y\rangle\leq\tfrac12\text{ whenever }x\ne y\text{ in }C\}.
\]
The maximum is finite. Multiplying points of $C$ by $2$ gives centers of disjoint-interior unit balls tangent to the unit ball centered at the origin.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Determine $\tau_n$ for every integer $n\geq1$, that is, determine the maximum number of pairwise nonoverlapping unit balls in $\mathbb R^n$ that can simultaneously touch a central unit ball. \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.04916}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
What is the largest possible number of equal spheres that can touch one central sphere without overlapping?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 1225 (GEO-030). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Henry Cohn and Anqi Li, \emph{Improved kissing numbers in seventeen through twenty-one dimensions}, arXiv:2411.04916v3 (2026), Section 1. \url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.04916}.
\end{description}
